==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|anglo-}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|NL.|Anglo-}}, from {{der|en|LL.|Anglī}}, {{m|la|Anglus||[[Anglo-Saxon]](s)}}, derived from {{der|en|la|Anglia||[[England]]}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gem|-}}. Influenced by {{der|en|fr|anglo-}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|Angel-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæŋ.ɡləʊ/|a=UK}}

===Prefix===
{{en-prefix}}

# {{non-gloss definition|A combining form relating to [[England]] or, by extension, the [[United Kingdom]].}}
#: {{ux|en|[[Anglo-Indian#Adjective|'''Anglo-'''Indian]] rule brought [[:w:Indian Railways|railroads]], but also [[:w:Famine in India|some of the worst famines in history]].}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Anglo-'''German naval rivalry was a contributing factor to the [[:w:First World War|First World War]].}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=January|title=Crossing the Border|journal=Railway Magazine|page=2|text=The identification of the border between England and Scotland always has been a source of interest to railway travellers. For many years, however, the exact points north of Berwick and Carlisle at which the '''Anglo-'''Scottish main lines passed from one country to the other were not defined, but the erection of clear and unmistakable lineside signs has put the matter beyond all doubt.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|A combining form relating to the [[English]] or [[British]] people, culture, or livestock.}}
#: {{ux|en|The [[Anglo-Indian#Noun|'''Anglo-'''Indian]] [[:w:Cliff Richard|Cliff Richard]]'s 1952 "[[:w:Move It|Move It]]" is sometimes credited as the first British rock hit.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[:w:Thomas Stearns Eliot|T.S. Eliot]] was an [[anglophile|'''anglo'''phile]].}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Anglo-'''Kirghiz mares, now better known as [[:w:Novokirghiz|Novokirghiz]], produce more milk than representatives of either separate lineage.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|A combining form relating to the [[English]] language.}}
#: {{ux|en|Japanese [[anglophone#Noun|'''anglo'''phones]]{{mdash}}but not Chinese ones{{mdash}}often struggle with distinguishing /r/ and /l/.}}

